{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504426,"nonce":"7FDyY1tGPbcoGPK8O49Z1Yi7Z54=","target":"b23295047b535d372ecb7289f5d6e2995aee872e0de8b0e8c518d0c02ef466cd","message":"**What is wrong.** Two concrete defects, both consequences of the same gap.\n\nFirst, the cache entry `[\"settings\",\"clusters\"]` has **two contradictory freshness policies**. `features/alerts/FilterBar.tsx:61-65` declares it with `staleTime: 5 * 60_000`; `features/settings/SourcesSection.tsx:153-156` and `:333-336` declare the same key with the same `listClusters` fn and no `staleTime` at all, so they inherit the `15_000` default from `App.tsx:25`. `staleTime` is per-observer over a shared entry, so the cluster list refetches after 15 seconds when read from the settings screen and stays put for 5 minutes when read from the alert filter bar. Nobody can say which was intended, because the intent has no home.\n\nSecond, `features/settings/PoliciesSection.tsx:720-725` bypasses `qk` entirely: `queryKey: [\"policy-preview\", \"recent-alerts\"] as const`, fetching `listAlerts({ limit: 20, sort: \"-last_seen_at\" })`. That key is outside the `[\"alerts\"]` prefix, so the frame that actually changes it — `alert.upserted`, handled at `api/live.tsx:59` — never invalidates it, and neither does any of the 3 ack/comment/unsnooze mutations in `features/alerts/detail/Actions.tsx`. It is swept only by the `resync` catch-all at `api/live.tsx:83`, which is the \"your incremental state is not trustworthy\" path and not a routine event. Until then it turns over on its own 60-second timer.\n\n**The demonstration.** `api/keys.ts:4-7` states the rule the second defect breaks, in its own file header: *\"They are a factory rather than string literals because the live stream invalidates by prefix: an `alert.upserted` frame invalidates `[\"alerts\"]`, which covers every filtered list without the stream needing to know what filters are on screen.\"* `PoliciesSection.tsx:721` is a filtered alert list that is not under that prefix. `api/keys.ts:1` says *\"Query keys, in one place\"* — and they are. The fetch functions are in a second place, `api/endpoints.ts`, and **nothing binds the two**: there is no `hooks/` or `queries/` directory anywhere in `web/src`, and every one of the **28 `useQuery`** and **19 `useMutation`** call sites in application code re-pairs a key with a fn by hand. `qk.settings.clusters()` is re-paired 3 times, `qk.settings.channels()` twice (`ChannelsSection.tsx:96`, `PoliciesSection.tsx:225`), `qk.settings.sources()` twice (`SourcesSection.tsx:148`, `TuningSection.tsx:262`). The clusters divergence is what re-pairing by hand produces on the third copy; the other two pairs are consistent only by luck.\n\nInvalidation duplicates the same way. Of **22 `invalidateQueries` calls** in application code, **8 target `qk.alerts.all()` across 3 owners**: `api/live.tsx:59,62,70,75`, `features/alerts/detail/Actions.tsx:136,270,371`, `routes/group-detail.tsx:92`. Within `Actions.tsx` alone, `AlertActions` names the call as a local `invalidate` helper at `:135-137` and then `AckDialog` (`:270`) and `CommentDialog` (`:371`) each write it out again 130 and 230 lines below.\n\n**Why it matters.** The clusters case is a live inconsistency where the correct answer is unknowable from the code. The `policy-preview` case is a stale list on a screen whose entire purpose is dry-running a routing policy against a real recent alert — an operator picks from a list up to a minute behind the alerts they are debugging. Both are the shape of defect that recurs at every new call site until one owner exists per resource.\n\n**Where.** The cheap half is deletion. `api/keys.ts` declares **5 key factories nothing calls**: `labels.values` (`:58`), `settings.sourceHealth` (`:63`), `settings.drills` (`:70`), `meta.me` (`:78`), `meta.version` (`:79`). `api/live.tsx:74` invalidates `qk.deliveries.all()`, which is the sole reference to that key — no query owns it, so a `delivery.updated` frame invalidates nothing on that line. And **14 exports have no caller** outside their own file and the generated `schema.d.ts`: `commentOnAlertGroup` (`api/endpoints.ts:365`), `updateCluster` (`:431`), `getSource` (`:439`), `deleteSource` (`:451`), `listDeliveryDrills` (`:484`), `getSourceHealth` (`:501`), `getChannel` (`:520`), `retryDelivery` (`:576`), `listEnrichers` (`:584`), `getStatsOverview` (`:588`), `getVersion` (`:651`), plus `Range` (`api/bounds.ts:109`), `rangeOf` (`api/bounds.ts:114`) and `prefersReducedMotion` (`design/theme.ts:91`).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** One `useX()` hook per resource owns its key, fn, options and invalidation; `qk` is the only source of query keys and `PoliciesSection.tsx:721` reads `qk.alerts.list(...)`; the clusters `staleTime` is one deliberate number stated once; and the 5 unused keys, the orphaned `qk.deliveries.all()` and the 14 dead exports are deleted.","files":null}]}